{
  "id": "80804",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Free Energy and Equilibrium",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The free energy change for a process may be viewed as a measure of its driving force. A negative value for Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " represents a finite driving force for the process in the forward direction, while a positive value represents a driving force for the process in the reverse direction. When Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " is zero, the forward and reverse driving forces are equal, and so the process occurs in both directions at the same rate (the system is at equilibrium)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "0365l8b2z6e31d4",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The reaction quotient, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is a convenient measure of the status of an equilibrium system. Recall that "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the numerical value of the mass action expression for the system, and that you may use its value to identify the direction in which a reaction will proceed in order to achieve equilibrium. When "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " is lesser than the equilibrium constant, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the reaction will proceed in the forward direction until equilibrium is reached and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " = "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Conversely, if "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Q"
              },
              {
                "text": " > "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the process will proceed in the reverse direction until equilibrium is achieved."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1ldmuygebxn9td5",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The free energy change for a process taking place with reactants and products present under nonstandard conditions, Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is related to the standard free energy change, Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": "°, according to the following equation"
              }
            ],
            "id": "z4j1s9s4l0lpdvo",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "fds7wog19swlo1o",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "hb6itsjtd0twzir",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bff5de076a20e3effebd4adc25b1b667/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [],
            "id": "48vq5lza1jtyb6w",
            "items": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Relationship between ΔG and ΔG°"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dt"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " ",
                        "type": "text"
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                    "src": "\\mathrm\\Delta G\\;=\\;\\mathrm\\Delta G^\\circ\\;+\\;RT\\;\\ln Q",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "R"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is the gas constant (8.314 J/K mol), "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "T"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is the kelvin or absolute temperature, and "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "Q"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is the reaction quotient."
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dd"
              }
            ],
            "type": "dl"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "xpsack6djqh83en",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "holbny5p4ncgybl",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bf/bff5de076a20e3effebd4adc25b1b667/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": " We may use this equation to predict the spontaneity for a process under any given set of conditions as illustrated in the example below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "wg7okehobdw836v",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2597957614",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "What is the free energy change for the process shown here under the specified conditions?"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ixorr45iott3su3",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "T"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 25 °C, "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "P"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "N"
                  },
                  {
                    "doublesub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 0.870 atm, "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "P"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "doublesub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 0.250 atm, and "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "P"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "NH"
                  },
                  {
                    "doublesub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 12.9 atm"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "zblgv4d10lxl0r4",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "yeplc7whl4so9b4",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "d9w9f2l4urdqdqh",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm{NH}}_3(g)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;3\\;{\\mathrm H}_2(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm N}_2(g)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm\\Delta G^\\circ\\;=\\;33.0\\;\\mathrm{kJ}/\\mathrm{mol}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The equation relating free energy change to standard free energy change and reaction quotient may be used directly:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "7eus4qpygyfyu69",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "999i2ukj2ywwl1l",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4ekotda243jalu9",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\mathrm\\Delta G\\;=\\;\\mathrm\\Delta G^\\circ\\;+\\;RT\\;\\ln Q",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "eemoqzzmhubdgcf",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "r7ub1rjvo1z0de8",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\mathrm\\Delta G\\;=\\;33.0\\frac{\\mathrm{kJ}}{\\mathrm{mol}}+\\left(8.314\\;\\frac{\\mathrm J}{\\mathrm{mol}\\;\\mathrm K}\\;\\times\\;298\\;\\mathrm K\\;\\times\\;\\ln\\frac{\\;(0.250^3)\\times0.870}{12.9^2}\\right)=\\;9680\\frac{\\mathrm J}{\\mathrm{mol}}\\;\\mathrm{or}\\;\\;9.68\\;\\mathrm{kJ}/\\mathrm{mol}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Since the computed value for Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "G"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is positive, the reaction is nonspontaneous under these conditions."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "6bq1jhfbeatjjg7",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1945642787",
            "selection": {
              "anchor": {
                "offset": 0,
